# Line endings, and why this file has to exist.
#
# The repository stores LF everywhere. A Windows clone with `core.autocrlf=true`
# — which is git-for-Windows' own default, and what a marketplace install ends up
# with — rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout. For prose that is
# harmless. For `bin/ab-hook` it is not: a shell script whose lines end in CR
# does not parse, and `bash` reports `$'\r': command not found` on every line.
#
# That matters more than a broken test run. `bin/ab-hook` is a HOOK. The two
# invariants this plugin is built on are that a hook always exits 0 and prints
# nothing or valid JSON, because a non-zero exit blocks the user's tool call. A
# CRLF `ab-hook` violates both on every single call.
#
# Today that is latent rather than live: `install.ps1` wires the Python entry
# point on Windows (`use_bash = not IS_WIN`), so `ab-hook` is never executed
# there. It goes live the moment a Windows machine with Git Bash or WSL picks the
# POSIX path — then the fast path in front of every tool call fails to parse.
# Measured on a Windows 10 work machine, 2026-08-10: every one of the 23 shell
# files in a fresh marketplace clone was CRLF, `ab-hook` included, and
# `bash tests/run.sh` died on line 15 before running an assertion.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides `core.autocrlf` per path, so this needs no renormalising —
# the blobs are already LF.

* text=auto

# Anything a shell has to parse, or Python has to exec as a script.
*.sh          text eol=lf
bin/ab-hook   text eol=lf
bin/agentbus  text eol=lf
bin/hook.py   text eol=lf
install.sh    text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the one place CRLF is the native convention, and the installer
# is the file a Windows user is most likely to open in an editor that cares.
*.ps1         text eol=crlf
